Day 240/250: Construct the Minimum Bitwise Array I

Problem Statement #3314

Problem Statement Explantion:

Given an array nums consisting of n prime numbers, you need to construct an array ans of length n such that for each index i, ans[i] is the smallest non-negative integer x that satisfies the equation:

x∨(x+1)=nums[i]
If no such x exists, set ans[i] = -1.

Solution Approach:

The core of this problem lies in understanding how the bitwise OR operator works with consecutive integers (x and x+1).

Key Insights:
The Even/Odd Rule: 
For any integer x, x∨(x+1) will always result in an odd number. 
This is because between x and x+1, one of them must have the least significant bit (LSB) set to 1.
Since 2 is the only even prime, and the result must be odd, there is no solution for nums[i]=2. 
Thus, ans[i]=−1.

The Bitwise Pattern:
Adding 1 to a binary number affects the rightmost string of contiguous 1s.
To find the minimum x, we need to find the rightmost block of ones in nums[i] and turn the most significant bit of that specific block into a 0.
Iterate through the input list nums.
Handle the Edge Case: If the number is 2, the answer is −1.

Bit Manipulation:

Find the first 0 bit from the right using the formula: mask = (~num) & (num + 1).
The bit to be flipped in the original number is one position to the right of this mask: bitToFlip = mask >> 1.
Perform an XOR between the original number and this bit to "turn off" that specific 1.
Store and Return the resulting array.

Here's the code:

import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public int[] minBitwiseArray(List<Integer> nums) {
        int n = nums.size();
        int[] ans = new int[n];
        
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int num = nums.get(i);
            
            if (num == 2) {
                ans[i] = -1;
            } else {
                int firstZeroBitMask = ((~num) & (num + 1));
                int bitToFlip = firstZeroBitMask >> 1;
                
                ans[i] = num ^ bitToFlip;
            }
        }
        
        return ans;
    }
}

Complexity:

Time Complexity: O(N)
Space Complexity: O(1)




